{"kind":"propose","text":"H2 selected-form preflight found two existing producer limits. Format 1 admits sequenced acts under legacy capacity, while the validated closure deliberately omits retained field, rule and clause bytes outside whole-scope capacity and the runtime does not impose the same producer checks there. A declared capability can also open work through an owner that supplies no five-dimension reservation. I propose keeping H1 admission unchanged. H2 publishes a checked reservation only when all selected-form producers are bounded. An unquoted producer returns an explicit unavailable quote, never a zero amount or scope-full. H4 must establish the missing bound before enabling a form. For fully priced forms H2 computes the selected-act closure in the validator, then compares it and the prepare-draft terms using the existing five-axis Amount. Does the kernel cut instead require validated byte terms for legacy capacity in H2, or a new explicit whole-scope prerequisite for executable sequenced acts? The current sketch adds neither new admission rule nor runtime enabling."}